>
> Altar camping has some pretty severe downsides. You
> tend to find really low quality armor, if any, at
> the beginning while the monsters are still puny,
> compared to the good stuff found in the Mines, and
> you level up far too fast. Your armor quality tends
> to lag the ferocity of the monsters you're
> attracting.

Mine town is the typical place I do my first round of
altar camping but on the way back up.  I don't avoid
leveling up by then so leveling up isn't a problem.  Also
since leveling up takes geometrically growing experience
points there aren't many levels to be gained in it.

For combat wombats I tend to do altar camping for artifacts
so any armor I'll get through initial inventories and deah
drops is icing on the cake.  For combat wombats I also
tend to do it for the AC points and other benefit after I've
accumulated several artifacts.

For spell casters I tend to go straight to altar camping
for books, AC points and the other random benfits.

In both cases the monsters tend to be dwarves and elves.
Mithril is as good as it gets, but for anywhere near that
phase that's as good as armor gets from monsters for a
very long while.  After the watch is toasted the soldiers in
both castles are the only reliable sources of armor and by
then the few dragons in both castles are the monsters of
interest.

> Yes, the protection you get balances out some of the
> armor problems, but it is randomly given, and so you
> risk getting really leveled up from kills, to match
> the 9 points of protection that Mines Town
> guarantees, before you find good armor, and leveled
> up to the point that walking down a few levels has a
> good chance of getting you killed because your AC is
> still sucky. In fact, you may get killed right on
> the altar by ranged weapons from ever more powerful
> monsters that arrive as you level up.

That's never been my experience.  Monsters are created
based on the average of depth and XP level.  Staying in
mine town on the way up means I'm already strong
compared to the level.  And leveling up means each level
up I go only adds half a level to the maximum monster
strength there.

As to 9 AC points, if I'm going to altar camp that will be
my goal.  For a combat wombat with a few /oCM it's a
lot.  For a spell caster with create monster 9 points is a
trivial number gotten as a side effect of a sizable library
of spellbooks.  My conclusion - altar camping works far
better for spell casters than for combat wombats so
combat wombats should likely go for artifacts anyways,
meaning protection racket works better for them.

For chaotic spell casters (wizards) altar camping for
books and AC points works so well I'll keep at it until
given finger of death.  A wizard carrying that when crowned
gets Stormbringer.  A wizard with a full library of blessed
books, lots of elven daggers thanks to the elves generated
in the camping, and Stormy as a melee backup is good to
go after DSM deeper down.

> Altar camping, IMO, is a far better bet _after_ the
> protection run, when you've got adequate armor, good
> AC already, and you're just happily, and more
> safely, busily gilding the lily.

Near as I can tell the armor gotten from altar camping on
the way back up through mine town can only be beaten
with DSM.  There are dragons in both castles.  But if I did
the arithmatic right dragons are such a high level they are
only likely to be created on levels 25 and deeper.  That is
often below the drawbridge Castle out of the range of
altar camping.  The exception to the rule of no better
armor than from dwarves and elves - robes from priests.
Good thing altar camping works best for chaotic spell
casters (special effects of rubbing hands together and
evil cackling).

> Of course, if you altar camp to a naked AC of -50
